orthant 1 language

- # `↯/s.105.4` — `Asn1.Error`
+ # `↯/s.105.4` — `Asn1.Error`

- Raised when Enigmatic cannot read a DER-encoded ASN.1 value.
+ Se lanza cuando Enigmatic no puede leer un valor ASN.1 codificado en DER.

- ## Cause
+ ## Causa

- DER (the Distinguished Encoding Rules) is the canonical subset of BER:
- every value has exactly one valid encoding. Enigmatic's decoder is
- strict, so anything BER permits but DER does not — indefinite lengths,
- overlong lengths and tag numbers, non-minimal integers, constructed
- strings, unordered sets — is an error rather than something to be
- tolerated. That strictness is what lets a value that was read back be
- re-encoded to the bytes it came from, which is what verifying a
- signature over a certificate's `TBSCertificate` depends on. The message
- is `could not process the ASN.1 value because $reason`, and every reason
- carries the byte offset at which the problem was found.
+ DER (las Distinguished Encoding Rules) es el subconjunto canónico de BER: cada valor tiene
+ exactamente una codificación válida. El decodificador de Enigmatic es estricto, así que todo lo
+ que BER permite pero DER no —longitudes indefinidas, longitudes y números de etiqueta
+ sobredimensionados, enteros no mínimos, cadenas construidas, conjuntos sin ordenar— es un error
+ y no algo que tolerar. Esa rigidez es lo que permite que un valor leído vuelva a codificarse en
+ los bytes de los que procede, que es de lo que depende verificar una firma sobre el
+ `TBSCertificate` de un certificado. El mensaje es
+ `could not process the ASN.1 value because $reason`, y cada motivo lleva el desplazamiento en
+ bytes en el que se encontró el problema.

- ## Variants
+ ## Variantes

- ```tabular
+ ```tabular

- column  Code
- column  Reason
- column  Meaning
- column  Message
- column  Resolution
- row  `↯/s.105.4.1`  `Truncated`  The input ended before the current value did.  `[↯/s.105.4.1] could not process the ASN.1 value because the input was truncated at byte 42`  Confirm the input is complete; a PEM body that was cut short, or a length prefix read from the wrong offset, are the usual causes.
- row  `↯/s.105.4.2`  `IndefiniteLength`  A value used BER's indefinite-length form, which DER forbids.  `[↯/s.105.4.2] could not process the ASN.1 value because an indefinite length was found at byte 1`  Re-encode the input as DER; `openssl asn1parse` will show where the indefinite lengths are.
- row  `↯/s.105.4.3`  `NonMinimalLength`  A length was not written in the fewest possible bytes: either the long form was used for a length below 128, or its leading byte was zero.  `[↯/s.105.4.3] could not process the ASN.1 value because an overlong length was found at byte 1`  Re-encode the input as DER.
- row  `↯/s.105.4.4`  `NonMinimalTag`  A tag number below 31 was written in the high-tag-number form, or a high tag number had a leading zero group.  `[↯/s.105.4.4] could not process the ASN.1 value because an overlong tag number was found at byte 1`  Re-encode the input as DER.
- row  `↯/s.105.4.5`  `NonMinimalInteger`  An integer carried a redundant leading `0x00` or `0xFF` byte. DER requires the shortest two's-complement representation.  `[↯/s.105.4.5] could not process the ASN.1 value because an overlong integer was found at byte 0`  Re-encode the input as DER.
- row  `↯/s.105.4.6`  `EmptyInteger`  An integer had no content octets; ASN.1 requires at least one.  `[↯/s.105.4.6] could not process the ASN.1 value because an integer with no content was found at byte 0`  Check the producer of the input: a zero must be encoded as the single byte `0x00`.
- row  `↯/s.105.4.7`  `Overflow`  A length or tag number was too large to represent: a length needing more than four bytes, a length above `Int.MaxValue`, or a tag number above `Int.MaxValue`.  `[↯/s.105.4.7] could not process the ASN.1 value because an unrepresentable length was found at byte 1`  Values this large are almost always a sign that the input is not DER at all, or that parsing began at the wrong offset.
- row  `↯/s.105.4.8`  `Trailing`  A complete value was read, but bytes remained after it. A DER document holds exactly one value.  `[↯/s.105.4.8] could not process the ASN.1 value because unexpected trailing bytes were found from byte 2`  If the input holds several concatenated values — a certificate chain, for instance — split it before decoding.
- row  `↯/s.105.4.9`  `InvalidUtf8`  A string's content octets were not valid UTF-8.  `[↯/s.105.4.9] could not process the ASN.1 value because invalid UTF-8 was found at byte 12`  Check the encoding of the source text; ASN.1 string types other than `UTF8String` may carry a different character set.
- row  `↯/s.105.4.10`  `ReservedTag`  Universal tag number 0 was found; it is reserved by X.690.  `[↯/s.105.4.10] could not process the ASN.1 value because the reserved tag number 0 was found at byte 0`  The input is not valid ASN.1; check that decoding began at the start of a value.
- row  `↯/s.105.4.11`  `BadOid`  An object identifier was malformed: it had no content, or its final subidentifier's continuation bit was set, or a subidentifier began with the padding byte `0x80`.  `[↯/s.105.4.11] could not process the ASN.1 value because a malformed object identifier was found at byte 3`  Re-encode the input as DER.
- row  `↯/s.105.4.12`  `OidArcOverflow`  An object identifier arc was too large for an `Int`. Arcs this large occur under the UUID arc `2.25`, which Enigmatic's `Asn1.ObjectId` does not yet model.  `[↯/s.105.4.12] could not process the ASN.1 value because an arc too large for Int was found at byte 3`  Read the value as raw bytes for now; wider arcs are a planned extension.
- row  `↯/s.105.4.13`  `UnsortedSet`  The members of a `SET` were not in ascending order of their encodings, as X.690 §11.6 requires.  `[↯/s.105.4.13] could not process the ASN.1 value because the set at byte 5 was not in ascending order`  Re-encode the input as DER; encoders that emit BER often leave `SET` members in their declaration order.
- row  `↯/s.105.4.14`  `BadTime`  A `UTCTime` or `GeneralizedTime` was not in DER's single permitted form — `YYMMDDHHMMSSZ` and `YYYYMMDDHHMMSSZ` respectively — or named a date that does not exist.  `[↯/s.105.4.14] could not process the ASN.1 value because a malformed time value was found at byte 0`  Re-encode the input as DER: fractional seconds, offsets from UTC and omitted seconds are all forbidden.
- row  `↯/s.105.4.15`  `BadBoolean`  A boolean's content octet was neither `0x00` nor `0xFF`. BER allows any non-zero byte to mean `true`; DER does not.  `[↯/s.105.4.15] could not process the ASN.1 value because a boolean with the content byte 1 was found at byte 0`  Re-encode the input as DER.
- row  `↯/s.105.4.16`  `BadLength`  A value's length was not one its type permits: a `NULL` with content, a boolean whose length was not 1, or a `BIT STRING` with no content.  `[↯/s.105.4.16] could not process the ASN.1 value because the tag 5 had the invalid length 1 at byte 0`  Check the producer of the input.
- row  `↯/s.105.4.17`  `BadUnusedBits`  A `BIT STRING`'s leading octet declared more than seven unused bits, declared unused bits for an empty string, or the bits it declared unused were not zero.  `[↯/s.105.4.17] could not process the ASN.1 value because a bit string declaring 8 unused bits was found at byte 0`  Re-encode the input as DER.
- row  `↯/s.105.4.18`  `NotPrimitive`  A type that DER requires to be primitive — the string types, integers, `BIT STRING`, `OCTET STRING`, `NULL`, object identifiers and the two time types — was encoded in constructed form.  `[↯/s.105.4.18] could not process the ASN.1 value because the tag 4 was encoded in constructed form at byte 0`  Re-encode the input as DER; segmented (constructed) strings are a BER feature.
- row  `↯/s.105.4.19`  `NotConstructed`  A `SEQUENCE` or `SET` was encoded in primitive form.  `[↯/s.105.4.19] could not process the ASN.1 value because the tag 16 was encoded in primitive form at byte 0`  The input is not valid ASN.1; check that decoding began at the start of a value.
+ column  Código
+ column  Motivo
+ column  Significado
+ column  Mensaje
+ column  Solución
+ row  `↯/s.105.4.1`  `Truncated`  La entrada terminó antes que el valor en curso.  `[↯/s.105.4.1] could not process the ASN.1 value because the input was truncated at byte 42`  Confirma que la entrada está completa; las causas habituales son un cuerpo PEM que se cortó o un prefijo de longitud leído desde el desplazamiento equivocado.
+ row  `↯/s.105.4.2`  `IndefiniteLength`  Un valor usó la forma de longitud indefinida de BER, que DER prohíbe.  `[↯/s.105.4.2] could not process the ASN.1 value because an indefinite length was found at byte 1`  Vuelve a codificar la entrada como DER; `openssl asn1parse` mostrará dónde están las longitudes indefinidas.
+ row  `↯/s.105.4.3`  `NonMinimalLength`  Una longitud no se escribió con el menor número posible de bytes: o se usó la forma larga para una longitud inferior a 128, o su byte inicial era cero.  `[↯/s.105.4.3] could not process the ASN.1 value because an overlong length was found at byte 1`  Vuelve a codificar la entrada como DER.
+ row  `↯/s.105.4.4`  `NonMinimalTag`  Un número de etiqueta inferior a 31 se escribió en la forma de número de etiqueta alto, o un número de etiqueta alto tenía un grupo inicial a cero.  `[↯/s.105.4.4] could not process the ASN.1 value because an overlong tag number was found at byte 1`  Vuelve a codificar la entrada como DER.
+ row  `↯/s.105.4.5`  `NonMinimalInteger`  Un entero llevaba un byte inicial `0x00` o `0xFF` redundante. DER exige la representación en complemento a dos más corta.  `[↯/s.105.4.5] could not process the ASN.1 value because an overlong integer was found at byte 0`  Vuelve a codificar la entrada como DER.
+ row  `↯/s.105.4.6`  `EmptyInteger`  Un entero no tenía octetos de contenido; ASN.1 exige al menos uno.  `[↯/s.105.4.6] could not process the ASN.1 value because an integer with no content was found at byte 0`  Revisa quién produce la entrada: un cero debe codificarse como el byte único `0x00`.
+ row  `↯/s.105.4.7`  `Overflow`  Una longitud o un número de etiqueta era demasiado grande para representarse: una longitud que necesitaba más de cuatro bytes, una longitud superior a `Int.MaxValue` o un número de etiqueta superior a `Int.MaxValue`.  `[↯/s.105.4.7] could not process the ASN.1 value because an unrepresentable length was found at byte 1`  Valores tan grandes casi siempre indican que la entrada no es DER en absoluto, o que el análisis empezó en el desplazamiento equivocado.
+ row  `↯/s.105.4.8`  `Trailing`  Se leyó un valor completo, pero quedaron bytes después de él. Un documento DER contiene exactamente un valor.  `[↯/s.105.4.8] could not process the ASN.1 value because unexpected trailing bytes were found from byte 2`  Si la entrada contiene varios valores concatenados —una cadena de certificados, por ejemplo—, divídela antes de decodificarla.
+ row  `↯/s.105.4.9`  `InvalidUtf8`  Los octetos de contenido de una cadena no eran UTF-8 válido.  `[↯/s.105.4.9] could not process the ASN.1 value because invalid UTF-8 was found at byte 12`  Comprueba la codificación del texto de origen; los tipos de cadena de ASN.1 distintos de `UTF8String` pueden llevar otro juego de caracteres.
+ row  `↯/s.105.4.10`  `ReservedTag`  Se encontró el número de etiqueta universal 0, que X.690 reserva.  `[↯/s.105.4.10] could not process the ASN.1 value because the reserved tag number 0 was found at byte 0`  La entrada no es ASN.1 válido; comprueba que la decodificación empezó al principio de un valor.
+ row  `↯/s.105.4.11`  `BadOid`  Un identificador de objeto estaba mal formado: no tenía contenido, o su último subidentificador tenía activado el bit de continuación, o un subidentificador empezaba con el byte de relleno `0x80`.  `[↯/s.105.4.11] could not process the ASN.1 value because a malformed object identifier was found at byte 3`  Vuelve a codificar la entrada como DER.
+ row  `↯/s.105.4.12`  `OidArcOverflow`  Un arco de un identificador de objeto era demasiado grande para un `Int`. Arcos tan grandes aparecen bajo el arco UUID `2.25`, que el `Asn1.ObjectId` de Enigmatic aún no modela.  `[↯/s.105.4.12] could not process the ASN.1 value because an arc too large for Int was found at byte 3`  Por ahora, lee el valor como bytes en bruto; los arcos más anchos son una extensión prevista.
+ row  `↯/s.105.4.13`  `UnsortedSet`  Los miembros de un `SET` no estaban en orden ascendente de sus codificaciones, como exige X.690 §11.6.  `[↯/s.105.4.13] could not process the ASN.1 value because the set at byte 5 was not in ascending order`  Vuelve a codificar la entrada como DER; los codificadores que emiten BER suelen dejar los miembros de un `SET` en su orden de declaración.
+ row  `↯/s.105.4.14`  `BadTime`  Un `UTCTime` o un `GeneralizedTime` no estaba en la única forma que permite DER —`YYMMDDHHMMSSZ` y `YYYYMMDDHHMMSSZ`, respectivamente— o nombraba una fecha que no existe.  `[↯/s.105.4.14] could not process the ASN.1 value because a malformed time value was found at byte 0`  Vuelve a codificar la entrada como DER: las fracciones de segundo, los desfases respecto a UTC y la omisión de los segundos están todos prohibidos.
+ row  `↯/s.105.4.15`  `BadBoolean`  El octeto de contenido de un booleano no era ni `0x00` ni `0xFF`. BER permite que cualquier byte distinto de cero signifique `true`; DER no.  `[↯/s.105.4.15] could not process the ASN.1 value because a boolean with the content byte 1 was found at byte 0`  Vuelve a codificar la entrada como DER.
+ row  `↯/s.105.4.16`  `BadLength`  La longitud de un valor no era una de las que permite su tipo: un `NULL` con contenido, un booleano cuya longitud no era 1, o una `BIT STRING` sin contenido.  `[↯/s.105.4.16] could not process the ASN.1 value because the tag 5 had the invalid length 1 at byte 0`  Revisa quién produce la entrada.
+ row  `↯/s.105.4.17`  `BadUnusedBits`  El octeto inicial de una `BIT STRING` declaraba más de siete bits sin usar, declaraba bits sin usar para una cadena vacía, o los bits que declaraba sin usar no eran cero.  `[↯/s.105.4.17] could not process the ASN.1 value because a bit string declaring 8 unused bits was found at byte 0`  Vuelve a codificar la entrada como DER.
+ row  `↯/s.105.4.18`  `NotPrimitive`  Un tipo que DER exige que sea primitivo —los tipos de cadena, los enteros, `BIT STRING`, `OCTET STRING`, `NULL`, los identificadores de objeto y los dos tipos de tiempo— se codificó en forma construida.  `[↯/s.105.4.18] could not process the ASN.1 value because the tag 4 was encoded in constructed form at byte 0`  Vuelve a codificar la entrada como DER; las cadenas segmentadas (construidas) son una característica de BER.
+ row  `↯/s.105.4.19`  `NotConstructed`  Un `SEQUENCE` o un `SET` se codificó en forma primitiva.  `[↯/s.105.4.19] could not process the ASN.1 value because the tag 16 was encoded in primitive form at byte 0`  La entrada no es ASN.1 válido; comprueba que la decodificación empezó al principio de un valor.

